### The Big Bang

Everything starts at the big bang, they said ! Well, it’s pretty much the same thing with the computers when we are starting it ! 

When you powered up your machine, the CPU (central processing unit) use a firmware (= a software inside an hardware device) to load the operating system like windows or linux. It is called UEFI or BIOS in older systems who is going to search at a specific place the bootloader to start the OS.

The resources to get the OS starting, is located in the DISK, it is our permanent storage. What I called “disk” is a HDD (Hard Disk Drive) or now in more modern form a SSD (Solid-State Drive), but we are going to refer to this as the “disk”.

However, the CPU cannot execute programs directly from the disk at high speed. The data is read from the disk and loaded into RAM (Random Access Memory). Unlike the disk, RAM is volatile (it wipes when powered off), but it is drastically faster. This is where your running OS lives and where your programs are loaded when you launch them. 
That’s it, the big bang happen and I introduce you to this schema we will use for the rest of this post and goes more in details. Those 3 big blocks will be where all the magics are going to happen.
 
