### The center of the universe

Now let’s zoom in inside the CPU. The CPU is composed of a lot of different components, I will list them all, but do not be afraid ! Do not panic ! We are not going to use them all or need them all in our quests. It’s just a good thing to know some of them exists and be able to later go a little further in the explanations.

Branch predictor ; try to optimize the path that the code is going to follow and works with the IR
Instruction Register (IR) ; it is a small data box where stand the actual instruction
Program Counter (PC) ; get the address of the next instructions
Control Unit (CU) ; it’s the CU which decode and execute with sending the data at the right place like the ALU
Arithmetic Logic Unit (ALU) ;  it’s the part doing the mathematics / calculations needed
Floating Point Unit (FPU) ; it’s doing the calculations needed for floating point
SIMD ; vectorial calculation
Registers ; we are going to get back to them later on this post but they stock data, which are going to be used later.
L1 / L2 cache ; memory data preloaded (see below in this post)
Memory Management Unit (MMU) ; Translate Virtual Memory addresses to physical memory addresses (it’s hardware)
Translation Lookaside Buffer (TLB) ; is inside the MMU and is a sort of cache for the MMU.
Different buses ; they are the connection allowing the CPU to received and get data 

And all that are only present inside ONE core ! Which means when you have several core in your CPU, you’ll have multiple times what we described here. 

### Path to the memory 
One of the biggest subject in programming with performance in minds is to use the computer architecture the most efficient way. The CPU is built to be used in a specific way with memory and most people try to stick to this use. 

L1 and L2 cache holds data, loaded with the objective to retrieve them faster. L1 cache size is from 32 KB up to 128 KB while L2 cache is up to 512 KB to 2 MB (depending on machine). The computer really preload some data to be able to do that. Why ? it avoids getting the data from the RAM or the DISK (in the worst case) which take more time. 

But there is also a third cache (L3) which is shared through different core. The size of this cache is from 4 MB up to 96 MB, also depending on the CPU. 

One term, you’ll often heard about is “cache miss”, when you don’t find the data in the cache and need to load them from another source. The order is the following ; L1 -> L2 -> L3 -> RAM -> DISK, each one “costing” (= taking more time) more than the previous one. In computer we are talking about CPU cycle. Here is a little idea of the numbers; 
L1 : 1 to 5 cycles
L2 : 10 to 15 cycles
L3 : 40 to 60 cycles
RAM : 150 to 300 cycles
DISK : thousands to millions of cycles

But there are also other place where data is store at a lower level, smaller than any cache, but faster ! As fast as 0 cycle !!!

### Registers

Registers are small area of memory, which holds a certain size of data depending on the machine (32 bits or 64 bits on modern computers but can be lower in embedded world).
This is what we are going to manipulate while writing assembly code, because data will be store there and we will also manipulate them. 
From now on, I will assume we are on a 64 bit computers, I will not go too much on details about registers on this post as we are going to see them again later. All of them have a name and most of them have a specific goal / usage.
But here is a list ; 

RIP ; it’s our PC (program counter) seen before, it’s where we store the next instruction.
RAX ; stock the return values of function and is used to store accumulated value
RBX ; points to data but needs to be preserved by functions
RDI ;(Register Destination Index) points to destination for copy and used as 1st argument in functions
RSI ; (Register Source Index) used for copying memory and used for 2nd argument in functions
RDX ; stock the values for multiplications and divisions but also used for 3rd arguments in functions
RCX ; counter for loops but also as 4th arguments in functions
RBP ; points to the base of the stack frame
RSP ; point to the top of the stack frame
R8, R9 ;  5th and 6th arguments in function and general registers 
R10 to R15 ; general registers

You don’t have to know them by heart, you will use some of them when needed. With practice some are going to be very familiar with time. 
